      "text": "( Abdolali et al., 2017 ). As displacement of lower affinity compounds occurs, the compounds re-enter the liquid stream and effluent concen - trations may exceed the influent concentrations, leading to C/C 0 > 1, known as chromatographic effect, which cannot be taken into account by existing common descriptive models ( Benjamin and Lawler, 2013 ). Desorption most often applies to shorter chain PFAS, such as per - fluorobutanoic acid (PFBA), but it has also been observed in longer chain carboxylic acid PFAS compounds such as perfluorooctanoic acid (PFOA) ( McCleaf et al., 2017; Zaggia et al., 2016 ). Accounting for solute desorption is important in cases where the breakthrough models are used to inform full-scale system design. Media change-out at full-scale water treatment facilities is often based on longer-chain compounds, but those long run times can lead to desorp - tion of their shorter-chain homologs ( Belkouteb et al., 2020; Hohenstein and Bachmeier, 2015 ). Desorption also has critical implications for the levels of exposure to consumers, as effluent concentrations of certain compounds may be elevated above their influent concentrations, exposing consumers to even higher levels of some PFAS. Accurate full-scale system design needs to take these increased concentrations"
